    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "Use <strong>the + adjective</strong> for a whole group of people: <em>the rich = rich people, the young = young people, the elderly = older people, the unemployed = people without a job</em>. It works with some adjectives only: <em>rich, poor, young, old, elderly, sick, injured, unemployed, homeless</em>.",
        "tag": "the + adjective"
      },
      {
        "html": "The group is <strong>plural</strong>, so the verb is plural: <em>The elderly need help. The young are often online.</em> Don't add -s to the adjective: ✗ <em>the youngs</em>. For one person, use a noun: <em>an elderly man</em>, <em>a young woman</em>.",
        "tag": "Plural verb"
      },
      {
        "html": "Use <strong>one</strong> instead of a singular noun that we already know: <em>Which bag? The red one.</em> We use <em>one</em> after an adjective, after <em>this / that / which</em>, or after <em>a / another</em>: <em>I need a new one. Which one? This one.</em>",
        "tag": "one"
      },
      {
        "html": "Use <strong>ones</strong> instead of a plural noun: <em>I like these shoes, but I prefer the blue ones. The new ones are better.</em> Without a noun in the sentence before, we don't need <em>one</em>. For uncountable nouns, leave it out: <em>Do you want black tea or green?</em>",
        "tag": "ones"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>The elderly needs help.</em> → ✓ <em>The elderly need help.</em> · ✗ <em>He is a rich.</em> → ✓ <em>He is rich.</em> / <em>He is a rich man.</em> · ✗ <em>I like the red.</em> → ✓ <em>I like the red one.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Some nationality words work in the same way: <em>the French, the Dutch, the Spanish</em>. You can also use <em>the + adjective</em> for an idea. Then the verb is singular: <em>The unknown is often scary.</em> <em>The old</em> can sound less polite than <em>the elderly</em>, so most people use <em>the elderly</em> in normal speech. Several of these words are participle adjectives: <em>the injured, the unemployed</em>. See <a href=\"/ed-ing-adjectives\">-ed and -ing adjectives</a>. For the order of adjectives before a noun, see <a href=\"/adjective-order\">Adjective order</a>. For verb forms, see <a href=\"/present-simple\">Present simple</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
